\section{Which Context Affects the Parse?}

Whether an identifier has been declared and whether a token begins a declaration are different questions. The first can often be answered after a unique tree is built. The second may have to be answered before there is a unique tree at all. A useful frontend design separates constraints that determine tree shape from those that only validate a tree. Deferring the former without preserving the alternative shapes makes the parse depend on an arbitrary early guess; enforcing the latter in the parser makes every syntax client duplicate semantic analysis.

In \textit{Context-sensitive parsing for programming languages}, Bo\v{s}tjan Slivnik surveys devices such as semantic predicates and data-dependent grammars, and studies a deterministic context-sensitive reduction system. His parser uses a reduction automaton to locate the next reduction under the system's explicit selection discipline. This is a more restricted claim than an efficient parser for arbitrary type-1 grammars. A scoped predicate added to an otherwise context-free grammar is likewise not the same formalism as a context-sensitive grammar. The predicate delegates a decision to a supplied environment; the grammar alone no longer describes that decision.

\section{Worked Example: A Name That Changes Role}

Consider a C-like fragment in which \texttt{T * x;} is a pointer declaration if \texttt{T} denotes a type, and a multiplication statement if \texttt{T} denotes a value. This toy language uses one namespace with nested scopes; a local value can shadow a type declaration.
\begin{verbatim}
type T = Int;
T * x;
{
  let T = 2;
  T * x;
}
T * z;
\end{verbatim}
The first occurrence of \texttt{T * x;} produces a declaration node and introduces the value name \texttt{x}. The occurrence inside the block produces a multiplication node with references to the inner \texttt{T} and the outer \texttt{x}. The final statement is again a declaration, because exiting the block restores the visibility of the type. These are assertions about tree shape and binding; whether the multiplication is well typed is a separate test.

A lexer can return a neutral identifier token with spelling and source span. At the decision point the parser asks for the nearest visible binding of \texttt{T} in the environment for that token. One can also classify such tokens in a parser-aware lexer, but lookahead is then a hazard: a token classified before a declaration takes effect can be wrong by the time it is consumed. Delayed classification, or explicit invalidation and reclassification across environment changes, prevents that stale decision. The language must specify at what declaration point the new binding becomes visible; a parser should not accidentally make that rule depend on when it requests lookahead.

\Cref{fig:visual-133} tracks two parses with identical token spellings.
The deciding input is the nearest binding of \texttt{T}, not a
different lexer token.

