\section{Making the Dependency Visible}

The attribute-grammar treatment in Ken Slonneger and Barry Kurtz's \textit{Formal Syntax and Semantics of Programming Languages} describes how to augment a context-free tree with context conditions. An inherited environment can flow into each statement, while a synthesized environment returned by a declaration flows to the next statement. \textit{Formal Languages and Compilation}, by Crespi Reghizzi, Breveglieri, and Morzenti, likewise treats attribute grammars as semantic computations on syntax. These presentations suggest an explicit interface even in an ordinary handwritten parser: pass the environment in, return any scope changes, and identify which facts actually governed a parse choice. An attribute evaluator that expects a unique tree cannot, on its own, choose that tree before the competing shapes have been represented.

For speculative or generalized parsing, use branch-local environments. A failed declaration branch must not leave a binding visible to the successful expression branch. For incremental parsing, reusing a decision requires more than unchanged bytes: either the incoming environment is unchanged or the particular lookups used by the decision still have the same outcomes. A cached unsuccessful lookup is a dependency too; inserting a declaration can turn it into a successful one.

As a regression, edit \texttt{let T = 2;} to \texttt{let U = 2;} and verify that the second occurrence changes to a declaration while the surrounding decisions do not. Then undo the edit and compare with a fresh parse. The dependency boundary is the same issue developed in \href{https://github.com/Chubek/TheTomeOfLangDev/blob/HEAD/dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/22-incremental-compiler-as-a-reactive-system.md}{Markdown Dossier Chapter~22}, and Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-137} considers how to preserve it even when an edit leaves a declaration incomplete.

