\section{Validating a Cache Entry Across a Scope Edit}

Consider a parser with a different contextual form, \texttt{open M;}.
If the nearest binding of \texttt{M} is a module, the form becomes an
import; if no binding exists, the language treats it as an ordinary
command with an identifier argument. A parser cache can reuse the
command even when some unrelated declaration changed. Assume immutable
scope snapshots, a stable grammar, and that the decision procedure
reads the environment only through an instrumented lookup. Give the
cache a key containing the stable token slice and parser entry state.
The output is a syntax choice and an unapplied declaration delta, not
a mutation of the supplied scope. Source locations can be reattached
to the stable token identities when the tree is reused.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input key $k$, scope snapshot $E$, cache $C$
  \Output node and declaration delta
  \State $r \gets C[k]$
  \If{$r$ exists}
    \State $ok\gets\mathsf{true}$
    \ForAll{$(q,v)$ in $r.reads$}
      \Comment{Replay negative reads too}
      \State $ok\gets ok \land (\Call{Lookup}{E,q}=v)$
    \EndFor
    \If{$ok$}
      \State \Return $r.result$
    \EndIf
  \EndIf
  \State $(result,reads)\gets\Call{ParseAndRecord}{k,E}$
  \State $C[k]\gets(result,reads)$ \Comment{No scope mutation}
  \State \Return $result$
\end{sudocode}

Start with a file containing only \texttt{open M;} in an empty scope.
Parsing records the read $(M,\mathsf{missing})$ and returns a command
with an empty delta. Add \texttt{let n = 3;} immediately before the
form. The parser entry state and token slice for the form are unchanged;
the lookup still says missing, so the same command is returned.
Instead add a module declaration with identity $17$ for \texttt{M}.
Replaying the read now returns $(\mathsf{module},17)$, so the parser
replaces the cached command with an import of module $17$. The caller
applies the returned delta only to the committed successor scope.

The invariant is that every environment branch on the cached parse
path would make the same decision in the new snapshot. Determinism
then ensures that replay would not take a new path with additional
reads. Compare full identities if the output contains resolved
references, rather than merely comparing symbol kinds. If an import
delta enumerates exports, the export list is another dependency;
the module name alone cannot validate it. With $r$ recorded reads
and maximum scope depth $d$, replay costs $O(rd)$ if each frame has
constant-time lookup. Use complete reparse when the grammar version
changes or a contextual operation has not been instrumented.
